WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.710
DINA PORTMAN: Hi, Martin.

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.820
Are you busy?

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:03.930
MARTIN OMANDER: I'm free.

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:05.472
DINA PORTMAN: I was
wondering if it's

00:00:05.472 --> 00:00:08.370
possible to call a private Cloud
Function from a Google Compute

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:09.555
Engine instance.

00:00:09.555 --> 00:00:10.680
MARTIN OMANDER: Yes, it is.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:11.670
I'll show you how.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:15.058
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:24.180
So Dina, out of
curiosity, why do you

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.860
want to know about calling
a private Cloud Function?

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:28.290
DINA PORTMAN: OK.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:30.840
After the big HR outage
we had last week,

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.960
our manager wants us to move
the on-prem HR application

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:38.820
to the cloud, and she wants
us to re-architect it using

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.070
service-oriented architecture.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:46.540
I'm a little overwhelmed
over where to start.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:50.460
I have a feeling we'll have to
use private Cloud Functions.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.500
MARTIN OMANDER: OK,
let's think about it.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.870
We have two main challenges.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:56.760
How do we move the
app to the Cloud,

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.960
and then how do we migrate to a
service-oriented architecture?

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:01.890
DINA PORTMAN: Right.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:06.220
But remember, our HR
application is written in COBOL.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:10.600
It's not exactly a runtime that
cloud providers usually cover.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:13.560
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah, good point.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:14.175
I know.

00:01:14.175 --> 00:01:16.590
Give me some Mountain
Dew and six weeks,

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:19.530
and I'll rewrite the
whole thing in Python.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:24.210
DINA PORTMAN: [LAUGHS] I'm
not sure that's a good idea.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.730
We know from the DORA
research that applications

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:29.340
are more stable and
have fewer incidents

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.950
if we roll out small
changes frequently.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:35.820
Six weeks of refactoring
and one big push

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.580
sounds like a
recipe for a crash.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:40.770
With that many lines
of code, how are you

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:43.170
even going to isolate the bugs?

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:46.490
And the system won't stand
still in the meantime.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.340
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah, good point.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:50.850
Cooler heads prevail.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:52.810
Oh, here's another idea.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:56.250
What if we load the app
as-is into a virtual machine,

00:01:56.250 --> 00:02:00.120
and then we peel off services
over time that we can put

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.280
into Google Cloud Functions?

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.880
For instance, we could start
with the vacation requester.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.100
DINA PORTMAN: I love that idea.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.770
I think it'll work,
but we'll have

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:13.350
to use private Cloud
Functions, because we

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:16.560
don't want just anyone messing
with our vacation requests.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:17.520
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.160
So now I see why
you want to know

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.830
how to get a virtual machine
to talk to a private Cloud

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:23.640
Function.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:24.240
All right.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.690
So our plan is to migrate
the app to a virtual machine

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:31.080
and then break out one
service to a Cloud Function.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:33.990
But first, we need to validate
that the virtual machine is

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:36.773
able to call a private
Cloud Function.

00:02:36.773 --> 00:02:38.940
DINA PORTMAN: I say let's
test it out with a default

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:42.480
hello, world function, and I can
do a curl to the Cloud Function

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.790
URL from the virtual machine.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.650
MARTIN OMANDER:
Sounds good to me.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:47.610
DINA PORTMAN: OK.

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.220
I will start by making
the Cloud Function.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:53.670
Here we go--

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:56.160
Create.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.010
And to be extra
special sure, I'm

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:02.370
going to set it to
Require Authentication.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:03.960
I'll save that.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:10.590
And to internal traffic only.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:13.140
I want to use
internal traffic only

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.480
to block any external
requests, and I

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.580
want to require
authentication to ensure

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:21.960
no unauthorized internal apps
have access to my service.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:25.590
MARTIN OMANDER: All
right, sounds good.

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:27.090
Looks like your
function deployed.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:31.050
And now click it and let's check
the Trigger link to make sure

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:34.130
that it denies our request.

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:35.430
DINA PORTMAN: Ah, yes.

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:38.550
It's blocking my
request, so we know

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:41.550
that our settings are working.

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:44.730
MARTIN OMANDER: An error message
we wanted to see-- excellent.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:47.730
So now, let's send it
some internal traffic

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:51.150
from a virtual machine.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:51.990
DINA PORTMAN: OK.

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:55.150
I'll create a new
virtual machine.

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:56.010
Here we go--

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:59.340
Create instance.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:04.500
Just leave all these defaults
on, scroll to the bottom,

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.823
and I'm going to use the
default service account.

00:04:07.823 --> 00:04:09.240
MARTIN OMANDER:
Why does it matter

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:12.130
which service account you use?

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:13.810
DINA PORTMAN: Oh, good question.

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:15.850
For the authentication,
we need to use

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:17.589
a service account
that has permission

00:04:17.589 --> 00:04:19.269
to invoke our function.

00:04:19.269 --> 00:04:22.150
The default compute service
account has this by default

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:25.180
if you're also using the default
settings on the Google Cloud

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:26.480
Function side.

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:30.040
Of course, these permissions
are all configurable,

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:32.650
but we can talk about
that another time.

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:33.640
MARTIN OMANDER: Got it.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:34.450
DINA PORTMAN: OK.

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:38.800
I'm going to SSH into it
and try pinging my function.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:39.910
First, I need my URL.

00:04:42.445 --> 00:04:43.660
Let's copy this.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:44.602
Copy Link.

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:53.030
And we're just going
to do a curl call here.

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:53.530
curl.

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:10.870
Oh, it's saying
forbidden, 403 error.

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:12.130
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah, right.

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:15.850
Don't forget the
Authorization header.

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:18.100
DINA PORTMAN: Oh, yeah.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:18.775
I forgot.

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:23.700
OK, so I'm going
to run this again.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:28.070
Just going to add my
Authorization header.

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:29.050
It worked.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:30.800
MARTIN OMANDER: So now
you're able to call

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:33.410
from a virtual machine
in the same project

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:35.750
if you have the
Authorization header.

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:38.000
Just to be sure, let's
try the same command

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:40.640
from your desktop machine.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.320
DINA PORTMAN: 403.

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:43.190
Awesome.

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:46.280
We can access the function from
inside the virtual machine,

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:49.160
but not from the
public internet.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:50.750
MARTIN OMANDER: Wonderful.

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:53.360
How would we do this from
inside an application,

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:55.930
instead of using curl?

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:57.530
DINA PORTMAN: That's
a great question.

00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:00.080
For service-to-service
requests, we can either

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:02.630
write a direct call
to the metadata server

00:06:02.630 --> 00:06:04.970
or we can use one of
the Google Auth Client

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:07.760
libraries, which will
construct this call for you.

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:13.100
The client libraries cover C#,
Go, Java, Node.js, PHP, Python,

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:14.780
and Ruby.

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:16.340
MARTIN OMANDER: Amazing.

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:17.960
I think we have
enough information

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:20.850
to make our project proposal.

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:23.280
DINA PORTMAN: Oh,
man, I completely

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.267
forgot about the COBOL.

00:06:25.267 --> 00:06:26.100
MARTIN OMANDER: Ugh.

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:28.763
We better get working on
that vacation requester.

00:06:28.763 --> 00:06:30.180
We're going to
need it after this.

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:33.530
[MUSIC PLAYING]

